1 NOTE === GEDCOM Note === &lt;p&gt;John was born on June 9, 1971 and died on December 1, 1853. On March 3, 1816 he married Elizabeth Moore (1800-1878) daughter of Dr. DAvid and Elizabeth Essex Moore of Nort
2 CONC h River. John was a balcksmith by trade and resided with his family in a house of the property where the new Fire Hall is situated on Kent Street, Charlottetown. while the robinsons liver there, Mason
2 CONC ic meetins were held in one of the upstair rooms, which was kept solely for that purpose. Later as their sons gres to manhood and expressed a desite to becomre farmers, John purchased McAusland or Pet
2 CONC er's Island, later to become known as 'Robinson's Lisland,' situated north of Rustico. This little island was a gem, in that it boasted two miles of sand dunes for pasture land, a growth of soft and h
2 CONC ard wood, cranberry bogs and two hundred acres suitable for farming. It also had a house and outbuildings. Here John settled his sons Peter Wendover and John williamm and it reamined in teh family unt
2 CONC il 1937, when it was sold to the Government for a National Park.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;p&gt;About 1848 John robinson moved to Mount Pleasnad, Lot 12 where he beca
2 CONC me involved in shipbuilding. while on a business trip to st. Pierre, a French possession south of Newfoundland, a disastrous fire brok out threatening total destruction. mr. Robinson organized a fire 
2 CONC brigade and after strenuous labour succeeded in brining the fire under control. for this heroic deed the French government presented him with a medal inscribed thus: "A JOHN ROBINSON RECOMPENSE DE SON
2 CONC  DEVOUMENT LORS DU 11 OCTOBRE 1844 A ST PIERRE DE TERRE NEUVE". To John robinson as a reward for his great and voluntary help on the occasion of the great fire as Isle St. Pierre on 11 October 1844.:&
2 CONC lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;p&gt;John died at Mount Pleasand in Lot 12 of a hernia caused by a kick from a horse. He is buried in St. James Church Cemetery, Port Hill, 
2 CONC Prince Edward Island.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
